   "id": "bava_metzia.19a.14",
   "daf": "19a",
   "sentenceId": 13,
   "he": "דְּיָיתֵיקֵי מַתָּנָה וְכוּ׳. תָּנוּ רַבָּנַן: אֵיזוֹ הִיא דְּיָיתֵיקֵי – דָּא תְּהֵא לְמֵיקַם וְלִהְיוֹת שֶׁאִם מֵת נְכָסָיו לִפְלוֹנִי. מַתָּנָה – כֹּל שֶׁכָּתוּב בּוֹ ״מֵהַיּוֹם וּלְאַחַר מִיתָה״.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: If one found «wills [deyaytiki]» or deeds of «gift,» he should not return them. «The Sages taught» in a baraita: «What is» considered «a deyaytiki» and is collected by the designated recipient after the death of the giver? It is a deed that states: «This deed will be to stand [da tehe lemeikam] and exist as» proof that «if» this person «dies, his property» is to be given «to so-and-so.» An ordinary deed of «gift,» by contrast, is «any» deed «in which it is written:» This gift is given «from today and after» the «death» of the giver."
  }
 ],
